[{:db/id 17592186133876 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-162e9632-ec96-53eb-bf5e-ab2bd97ae9e0" "question-ca50a185-e281-53e0-900c-05cc2ac6e7a5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133889 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-df216990-7a71-5841-8a5b-955dc6939d8d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9278ec55-4908-5c1c-8a74-640611473537" "question-5777673f-cba1-5775-ae01-f18d4531f239"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133696 :question/problem "![](images/d3/d37f2a445986b2aae60603fed3bef1b480d8f049dfe17a0cf26325ac2eb167d8.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F?$\n\nI. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$\n\nII. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F <                    0$\n\nIV. $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-162e9632-ec96-53eb-bf5e-ab2bd97ae9e0" :question/id #uuid "162e9632-ec96-53eb-bf5e-ab2bd97ae9e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-123172" :question/problem "Given that $F=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fadbbb52-eaa9-5960-9164-0e7ea16514af"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fadbbb52-eaa9-5960-9164-0e7ea16514af" :answer-field/id #uuid "fadbbb52-eaa9-5960-9164-0e7ea16514af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-88c4b453-3bda-58b8-8fbb-6a0d6916e90a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-507360f0-f40d-51b4-8d0a-a73fd33bb756" "answer-21305c88-eeaf-5820-af29-34723d616234" "answer-952b9fe9-4b9b-5eac-bcfa-e800b3b1094b" "answer-754db6a7-5db4-5e7b-9b14-ca330f6671e0" "answer-88c4b453-3bda-58b8-8fbb-6a0d6916e90a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-507360f0-f40d-51b4-8d0a-a73fd33bb756" :answer/id #uuid "507360f0-f40d-51b4-8d0a-a73fd33bb756" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+y)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21305c88-eeaf-5820-af29-34723d616234" :answer/id #uuid "21305c88-eeaf-5820-af29-34723d616234" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-y)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-952b9fe9-4b9b-5eac-bcfa-e800b3b1094b" :answer/id #uuid "952b9fe9-4b9b-5eac-bcfa-e800b3b1094b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2x-2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-754db6a7-5db4-5e7b-9b14-ca330f6671e0" :answer/id #uuid "754db6a7-5db4-5e7b-9b14-ca330f6671e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88c4b453-3bda-58b8-8fbb-6a0d6916e90a" :answer/id #uuid "88c4b453-3bda-58b8-8fbb-6a0d6916e90a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ca50a185-e281-53e0-900c-05cc2ac6e7a5" :question/id #uuid "ca50a185-e281-53e0-900c-05cc2ac6e7a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-105562" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(\\pi ,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(\\pi ,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(\\pi ,1) & =2(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+\\frac{\\pi }{1} \\\\  & =2(1)(1)+\\pi  \\\\  & =2+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6e9bf416-fabf-5d02-bf76-781ab322815f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6e9bf416-fabf-5d02-bf76-781ab322815f" :answer-field/id #uuid "6e9bf416-fabf-5d02-bf76-781ab322815f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-af6e0ce3-9249-535e-9fb2-893259204ce1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0c5b1d09-1da1-519d-9990-fff8f06520f0" "answer-55a8bdef-6d67-5f62-8f8a-3f474c61f4a6" "answer-af6e0ce3-9249-535e-9fb2-893259204ce1" "answer-2181d067-a072-5d02-8afa-0c943d7e22d4" "answer-b75001d5-d554-5e4b-9dfe-3ece61afe366"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c5b1d09-1da1-519d-9990-fff8f06520f0" :answer/id #uuid "0c5b1d09-1da1-519d-9990-fff8f06520f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55a8bdef-6d67-5f62-8f8a-3f474c61f4a6" :answer/id #uuid "55a8bdef-6d67-5f62-8f8a-3f474c61f4a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af6e0ce3-9249-535e-9fb2-893259204ce1" :answer/id #uuid "af6e0ce3-9249-535e-9fb2-893259204ce1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2+\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2181d067-a072-5d02-8afa-0c943d7e22d4" :answer/id #uuid "2181d067-a072-5d02-8afa-0c943d7e22d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b75001d5-d554-5e4b-9dfe-3ece61afe366" :answer/id #uuid "b75001d5-d554-5e4b-9dfe-3ece61afe366" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-df216990-7a71-5841-8a5b-955dc6939d8d" :question/id #uuid "df216990-7a71-5841-8a5b-955dc6939d8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123196" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+(y+z)^{2}\\,j+(x+z)^{2}\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{2}) \\\\  & =2(x+y)+2(y+z)+2(x+z) \\\\  & =4(x+y+z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-2,1,-1) & =4((-2)+(1)+(-1)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3452daaf-b0f7-51b4-a94c-d00ee7b3816f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3452daaf-b0f7-51b4-a94c-d00ee7b3816f" :answer-field/id #uuid "3452daaf-b0f7-51b4-a94c-d00ee7b3816f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4849233c-e677-553e-b525-8ce1ed84e89f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e36ebd6-f283-5b52-b5a9-f667a2f4b389" "answer-193ddeed-a793-53c6-ba1e-92e68b0f73f0" "answer-4849233c-e677-553e-b525-8ce1ed84e89f" "answer-cd60ac0d-9b78-5c69-bd7b-06d4c14bdb66" "answer-f47d0d8f-c2bd-587b-8969-e8a2b85184c9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e36ebd6-f283-5b52-b5a9-f667a2f4b389" :answer/id #uuid "6e36ebd6-f283-5b52-b5a9-f667a2f4b389" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-193ddeed-a793-53c6-ba1e-92e68b0f73f0" :answer/id #uuid "193ddeed-a793-53c6-ba1e-92e68b0f73f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4849233c-e677-553e-b525-8ce1ed84e89f" :answer/id #uuid "4849233c-e677-553e-b525-8ce1ed84e89f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd60ac0d-9b78-5c69-bd7b-06d4c14bdb66" :answer/id #uuid "cd60ac0d-9b78-5c69-bd7b-06d4c14bdb66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f47d0d8f-c2bd-587b-8969-e8a2b85184c9" :answer/id #uuid "f47d0d8f-c2bd-587b-8969-e8a2b85184c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9278ec55-4908-5c1c-8a74-640611473537" :question/id #uuid "9278ec55-4908-5c1c-8a74-640611473537" :question/math-academy-id "q-131196" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4bf8de7e8719545b3d8abf5dc9fb4bc0c5f71034dba7186b7d9822d9e8be044d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $x$-components decrease as we move horizontally towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7fd542c0-4d3b-584d-9d7b-3c0d0b5750ae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7fd542c0-4d3b-584d-9d7b-3c0d0b5750ae" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fd542c0-4d3b-584d-9d7b-3c0d0b5750ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-18f86bf7-5c17-5cb6-8c9a-25e3a30c5a86" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4bd6eba8-c859-5a8d-b522-f2c3b0ee5a65" "answer-4452f92a-63c8-5376-8e98-947775e29e69" "answer-26003085-8363-5ee2-8473-69f3ba602654" "answer-18f86bf7-5c17-5cb6-8c9a-25e3a30c5a86" "answer-8aff9788-bb7e-5bb2-9074-85e6460dae3e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bd6eba8-c859-5a8d-b522-f2c3b0ee5a65" :answer/id #uuid "4bd6eba8-c859-5a8d-b522-f2c3b0ee5a65" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4452f92a-63c8-5376-8e98-947775e29e69" :answer/id #uuid "4452f92a-63c8-5376-8e98-947775e29e69" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26003085-8363-5ee2-8473-69f3ba602654" :answer/id #uuid "26003085-8363-5ee2-8473-69f3ba602654" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18f86bf7-5c17-5cb6-8c9a-25e3a30c5a86" :answer/id #uuid "18f86bf7-5c17-5cb6-8c9a-25e3a30c5a86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8aff9788-bb7e-5bb2-9074-85e6460dae3e" :answer/id #uuid "8aff9788-bb7e-5bb2-9074-85e6460dae3e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5777673f-cba1-5775-ae01-f18d4531f239" :question/id #uuid "5777673f-cba1-5775-ae01-f18d4531f239" :question/math-academy-id "q-131195" :question/problem "![](images/39/39dfec02c787141d8a728381f4093dfe9cbf3fdbf996177ba6f24d973015dc5f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8def5ae-54bc-5cde-af27-af1ce38db335"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8def5ae-54bc-5cde-af27-af1ce38db335" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8def5ae-54bc-5cde-af27-af1ce38db335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b1ea2773-828b-51a5-b56b-8c1ce5f3d0df" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c14c9ebe-eb84-5d9c-8dd2-5d0a1d71c120" "answer-51328c89-8cdd-5ec3-96c7-541a1dd6496d" "answer-cd47d87e-fdba-55d2-b443-02e11d1f6d93" "answer-b1ea2773-828b-51a5-b56b-8c1ce5f3d0df" "answer-53e20d90-4e62-516b-ae3d-39f75e6f0b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c14c9ebe-eb84-5d9c-8dd2-5d0a1d71c120" :answer/id #uuid "c14c9ebe-eb84-5d9c-8dd2-5d0a1d71c120" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51328c89-8cdd-5ec3-96c7-541a1dd6496d" :answer/id #uuid "51328c89-8cdd-5ec3-96c7-541a1dd6496d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd47d87e-fdba-55d2-b443-02e11d1f6d93" :answer/id #uuid "cd47d87e-fdba-55d2-b443-02e11d1f6d93" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1ea2773-828b-51a5-b56b-8c1ce5f3d0df" :answer/id #uuid "b1ea2773-828b-51a5-b56b-8c1ce5f3d0df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53e20d90-4e62-516b-ae3d-39f75e6f0b26" :answer/id #uuid "53e20d90-4e62-516b-ae3d-39f75e6f0b26" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135114 :tutorial/content "Our goal now is to develop an intuitive understanding of what the divergence of a vector field measures.\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ and the point $S,$ shown below. Imagine that this vector field represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. \n\n![](images/a7/a785c84976242d699af11ab2203600e2007e95e35b60665ba9882c8f0f25bce3.png)\n\n \n\nWe can see from our diagram that $S$ acts like a **source**. You can think of this as an open faucet, pushing water onto the plane and away from itself. \n\nSince $S$ acts like a source, $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is nonzero. By convention, if a point $S$ acts like a source, the divergence is positive:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0\n$$\n\nIn this particular case, *every* point in our vector field acts as a source. So $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$ everywhere.\n\nIf we reverse the situation, we get the following:\n\n![](images/b4/b4c923bbde36568e4e2f36f7d388622a520d0799d558049f72d0194e0bd679f7.png)\n\n \n\nWe can see from our diagram that $T$ acts like a **sink**. You can think of this as an open plughole that sucks in the surrounding water.\n\nSince $T$ acts like a sink, $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is also nonzero. By convention, if a point $T$ acts like a sink, the divergence is negative:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0\n$$\n\nEvery point in our vector field acts like a sink in this case. So $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0$ everywhere.\n\nFinally, note that if $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere in the domain of some vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we say that $\\mathbf F$ is **solenoidal.**" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134010 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
